1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,456
A chair knocked over.

2
00:00:02,456 --> 00:00:04,954
A meal left half-eaten on the table.

3
00:00:04,954 --> 00:00:08,700
And in the lighthouse logbook, a keeper's final words:

4
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:09,811
God is over all.

5
00:00:10,785 --> 00:00:15,680
That is how the story of the Flannan Isles lighthouse is usually told.

6
00:00:15,680 --> 00:00:20,364
Three men, gone overnight, from a rock at the edge of the Atlantic.

7
00:00:21,339 --> 00:00:22,954
There's just one problem.

8
00:00:22,954 --> 00:00:28,443
The chair, the meal, and those famous last words don't appear in the official record.

9
00:00:31,919 --> 00:00:36,032
So what did the men who searched that lighthouse actually find?

10
00:00:36,032 --> 00:00:37,338
Let's open the file.

11
00:00:38,313 --> 00:00:47,048
About twenty miles west of the Isle of Lewis, in Scotland's Outer Hebrides, lies a small cluster of islands called the Flannans.

12
00:00:47,048 --> 00:00:50,323
Sailors sometimes called them the Seven Hunters.

13
00:00:51,298 --> 00:00:53,083
The largest is Eilean Mòr.

14
00:00:53,083 --> 00:00:59,533
Sheer cliffs on every side, barely any flat ground, and almost no shelter from the open ocean.

15
00:01:00,509 --> 00:01:03,368
Building a lighthouse there took years.

16
00:01:03,368 --> 00:01:10,774
Every stone, every tool and every sack of supplies had to be hauled up those cliffs from small boats.

17
00:01:10,774 --> 00:01:13,927
The light was finally lit in December 1899.

18
00:01:14,902 --> 00:01:19,442
A year later, in December 1900, three keepers were on the rock.

19
00:01:19,442 --> 00:01:21,893
James Ducat, the principal keeper.

20
00:01:21,893 --> 00:01:24,127
Thomas Marshall, his assistant.

21
00:01:24,127 --> 00:01:30,325
And Donald MacArthur, an occasional keeper, filling in for a regular man who was away.

22
00:01:31,302 --> 00:01:33,744
Life there followed a strict routine.

23
00:01:33,744 --> 00:01:38,432
Clean the lens, trim the wicks, wind the machinery, record the weather.

24
00:01:38,432 --> 00:01:45,232
Every few weeks a relief boat brought supplies and rotated one man ashore, whenever the sea allowed it.

25
00:01:46,207 --> 00:01:47,916
Keep one detail in mind.

26
00:01:47,916 --> 00:01:56,885
The island had two landing places, one on the east side and one on the west, because no single landing was safe in every wind.

27
00:01:56,885 --> 00:01:58,735
Remember the west landing.

28
00:01:58,735 --> 00:01:59,945
It matters later.

29
00:02:00,921 --> 00:02:08,606
On the night of the fifteenth of December 1900, a steamer called the Archtor passed the Flannans on its way to Leith.

30
00:02:08,606 --> 00:02:11,758
Her crew noticed that the light was not shining.

31
00:02:12,734 --> 00:02:17,391
For a lighthouse, darkness is the one thing that should never happen.

32
00:02:17,391 --> 00:02:20,968
A dead light is a danger to every ship on that coast.

33
00:02:21,943 --> 00:02:24,088
But news in 1900 moved slowly.

34
00:02:24,088 --> 00:02:31,308
The Archtor reached port days later, and the report took time to reach the Northern Lighthouse Board.

35
00:02:32,283 --> 00:02:36,534
The relief boat, the Hesperus, was due to make its scheduled trip.

36
00:02:36,534 --> 00:02:38,531
Bad weather kept it in harbour.

37
00:02:38,531 --> 00:02:42,524
It finally reached Eilean Mòr on the twenty-sixth of December.

38
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:46,974
Her captain, James Harvey, signalled to the island.

39
00:02:46,974 --> 00:02:50,653
Normally the keepers would come down to meet the boat.

40
00:02:50,653 --> 00:02:51,471
Nobody came.

41
00:02:51,471 --> 00:02:52,969
No one on the landing.

42
00:02:52,969 --> 00:02:54,059
No sign of life.

43
00:02:55,034 --> 00:02:58,257
The relief keeper, Joseph Moore, was put ashore.

44
00:02:58,257 --> 00:03:01,883
He climbed the long stairway up to the station, alone.

45
00:03:02,858 --> 00:03:04,856
The entrance gate was closed.

46
00:03:04,856 --> 00:03:06,235
The door was closed.

47
00:03:06,235 --> 00:03:10,921
Inside, the fire had gone out long before, and the rooms were empty.

48
00:03:11,896 --> 00:03:14,603
And in the hallway, there was something odd.

49
00:03:14,603 --> 00:03:17,616
Only one set of outdoor clothing was left behind.

50
00:03:17,616 --> 00:03:19,031
Three men were missing.

51
00:03:19,031 --> 00:03:22,044
Two of them had gone out dressed for bad weather.

52
00:03:22,044 --> 00:03:24,505
The third had gone out without his coat.

53
00:03:25,480 --> 00:03:28,919
Moore and the boat's crew searched the whole island.

54
00:03:28,919 --> 00:03:30,110
They found no one.

55
00:03:30,110 --> 00:03:30,837
Not a body.

56
00:03:30,837 --> 00:03:32,093
Not a single trace.

57
00:03:33,068 --> 00:03:39,818
A temporary crew stayed behind to keep the light burning, and the Hesperus carried the news back to Lewis.

58
00:03:39,818 --> 00:03:41,664
A telegram went to the Board.

59
00:03:41,664 --> 00:03:45,421
A dreadful accident, it said, had happened at the Flannans.

60
00:03:46,395 --> 00:03:51,794
Now, this is where the story you've probably heard starts to drift away from the record.

61
00:03:52,769 --> 00:04:00,472
Popular versions describe a meal left on the table and a chair knocked over, as if someone leapt up in panic.

62
00:04:00,472 --> 00:04:07,610
Those images seem to trace back to a poem, Flannan Isle, by Wilfrid Wilson Gibson, published in 1912.

63
00:04:07,610 --> 00:04:09,095
It's a haunting poem.

64
00:04:09,095 --> 00:04:11,286
But it is a poem, not a report.

65
00:04:12,260 --> 00:04:17,744
The official accounts we could find mention neither the meal nor the fallen chair.

66
00:04:18,718 --> 00:04:20,632
Then there's the logbook.

67
00:04:20,632 --> 00:04:24,076
Versions online quote dramatic final entries.

68
00:04:24,076 --> 00:04:27,521
Winds stronger than any seen in twenty years.

69
00:04:27,521 --> 00:04:28,899
One keeper crying.

70
00:04:28,899 --> 00:04:30,123
Another praying.

71
00:04:30,123 --> 00:04:31,042
And finally:

72
00:04:31,042 --> 00:04:32,726
Storm ended, sea calm.

73
00:04:32,726 --> 00:04:33,950
God is over all.

74
00:04:34,925 --> 00:04:39,808
But the official log's last entries are dated the thirteenth of December.

75
00:04:39,808 --> 00:04:47,232
Notes up to nine in the morning on the fifteenth were chalked onto a slate, waiting to be copied into the book.

76
00:04:47,232 --> 00:04:49,773
None of it matches those famous lines.

77
00:04:50,750 --> 00:04:55,404
The dramatic entries surfaced decades later, in magazine retellings.

78
00:04:55,404 --> 00:04:58,963
The Northern Lighthouse Board has no record of them.

79
00:04:59,937 --> 00:05:01,725
Think about what that means.

80
00:05:01,725 --> 00:05:08,108
The most quoted evidence in this case is a text that nobody can trace back to the lighthouse itself.

81
00:05:09,084 --> 00:05:12,431
So strip away the poem and the invented log.

82
00:05:12,431 --> 00:05:13,344
What's left?

83
00:05:13,344 --> 00:05:14,637
An empty station.

84
00:05:14,637 --> 00:05:16,387
A routine that stopped.

85
00:05:16,387 --> 00:05:17,833
One coat on a hook.

86
00:05:17,833 --> 00:05:19,430
And the west landing.

87
00:05:20,406 --> 00:05:26,228
On the twenty-ninth of December, Superintendent Robert Muirhead arrived to investigate.

88
00:05:26,228 --> 00:05:28,570
He had recruited these men himself.

89
00:05:28,570 --> 00:05:30,177
He knew them personally.

90
00:05:31,151 --> 00:05:35,308
He went straight to the west landing, and there he found damage.

91
00:05:35,308 --> 00:05:37,710
Iron railings were bent out of shape.

92
00:05:37,710 --> 00:05:39,009
A crane was damaged.

93
00:05:39,009 --> 00:05:47,387
A box of ropes and equipment, kept in a crevice high up the cliff, had been torn away, and its contents scattered over the rocks.

94
00:05:48,362 --> 00:05:50,948
This was not the work of ordinary waves.

95
00:05:50,948 --> 00:05:56,639
Whatever struck that landing reached far higher up the cliff than the sea normally does.

96
00:05:57,613 --> 00:06:04,197
In his report, dated the eighth of January 1901, Muirhead set out what he believed had happened.

97
00:06:04,197 --> 00:06:13,593
The men had gone down to the west landing to secure equipment in stormy weather, and they were carried away by an unexpectedly large sea.

98
00:06:14,567 --> 00:06:16,824
And the single coat fits that picture.

99
00:06:16,824 --> 00:06:19,317
Two men went down dressed for the weather.

100
00:06:19,317 --> 00:06:26,322
The third may have seen from above that something was going wrong, and run down to help without stopping for his coat.

101
00:06:27,298 --> 00:06:33,065
Then one large wave, rolling high up the cliff, could have taken all three in a moment.

102
00:06:33,065 --> 00:06:33,860
No struggle.

103
00:06:33,860 --> 00:06:35,451
No panic in the station.

104
00:06:35,451 --> 00:06:40,887
Just three men doing their job, in a place where the sea can rise without warning.

105
00:06:41,862 --> 00:06:42,663
Case closed?

106
00:06:42,663 --> 00:06:43,330
Not quite.

107
00:06:43,330 --> 00:06:44,797
Some questions remain.

108
00:06:44,797 --> 00:06:52,999
Why did all three men leave the station at the same time, when lighthouse practice expected someone to stay with the light?

109
00:06:53,973 --> 00:06:56,908
Some researchers suggest a sudden rogue wave.

110
00:06:56,908 --> 00:07:02,386
Others suggest one man was swept in, and the other two were lost trying to save him.

111
00:07:02,386 --> 00:07:04,603
We will probably never know which.

112
00:07:04,603 --> 00:07:06,560
Their bodies were never found.

113
00:07:07,536 --> 00:07:10,235
But here is what the record does tell us.

114
00:07:10,235 --> 00:07:16,557
The truth of the Flannan Isles is not a ghost, a sea monster, or a page of terrified last words.

115
00:07:16,557 --> 00:07:23,471
It's three ordinary men on an extraordinary rock, who went out to protect the light, and never came back.

116
00:07:24,447 --> 00:07:26,481
The legend gave them a mystery.

117
00:07:26,481 --> 00:07:29,039
The record gives them something better:

118
00:07:29,039 --> 00:07:31,401
the dignity of what really happened.

119
00:07:32,377 --> 00:07:36,167
The lighthouse on Eilean Mòr still shines today.

120
00:07:36,167 --> 00:07:38,141
It was automated in 1971.

121
00:07:38,141 --> 00:07:41,379
There are no keepers on the rock anymore.

122
00:07:42,355 --> 00:07:45,542
Every source we used is listed in the description.

123
00:07:45,542 --> 00:07:51,087
And if you know a mystery where the famous clue might be fake, tell us in the comments.

124
00:07:51,087 --> 00:07:53,000
It might become our next file.
